# Web Cache Poisoning via Unknown Header with User-Agent Targeting

## Executive Summary

A web cache poisoning vulnerability was identified in the application's caching infrastructure. An undocumented HTTP header (`X-Host`) was trusted by the application to construct an absolute URL for a JavaScript resource (`tracking.js`), while its value was not incorporated into the cache key.

Although the cache varied on the `User-Agent` header, it was possible to discover the victim's browser fingerprint and poison the corresponding cache entry. As a result, arbitrary JavaScript hosted on an attacker-controlled server was served to the victim, leading to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS).

